, 0.00846099853515625, -0.028167724609375, 0.001583099365234375, 0.017364501953125, 0.0165252685546875, -0.0292816162109375, 0.032562255859375, 0.0108184814453125, -0.0138702392578125, -0.0321044921875, 0.0140838623046875, 0.0163726806640625, 0.009796142578125, 0.01061248779296875, -0.01097869873046875, -0.0087738037109375, -0.02020263671875, 0.01232147216796875, -0.0311279296875, 0.002384185791015625, -0.0013265609741210938, -0.0198211669921875, 0.01549530029296875, -0.01491546630859375, 0.00463104248046875, -0.0134429931640625, 0.0272216796875, -0.032958984375, 0.019287109375, -0.0169830322265625, -0.007080078125, -0.007053375244140625, 0.0213165283203125, 0.001678466796875, -0.007457733154296875, 0.0150146484375, 0.0186920166015625, 0.0159759521484375, 0.0008969306945800781, 0.0198974609375, -0.01519012451171875, -0.0012636184692382812, 0.0109710693359375, 0.0175018310546875, -0.017059326171875, -0.01261138916015625, 0.0084228515625, -0.05242919921875, -0.0254364013671875, 0.047454833984375, -0.0177154541015625, 0.0035533905029296875, 0.0279693603515625, 0.008880615234375, 0.0011882781982421875, 0.02044677734375, 0.0180511474609375, 0.00681304931640625, -0.0304412841796875, -0.0015773773193359375, -0.00560760498046875, 0.007617950439453125, 0.004840850830078125, 0.0132598876953125, -0.0020465850830078125, 0.026519775390625, 0.040863037109375, -0.041595458984375, 0.04144287109375, -0.029266357421875, -0.00885009765625, 0.0164337158203125, 0.00383758544921875, -0.014862060546875, 0.00524139404296875, -0.01226806640625, 0.0175018310546875, -0.03521728515625, -0.0150299072265625, 0.01436614990234375, -0.02587890625, -0.004062652587890625, 0.00896453857421875, -0.01044464111328125, -0.0357666015625, 0.06988525390625, 0.0098876953125, 0.0020923614501953125, -0.00537109375, 0.044647216796875, -0.035491943359375, 0.00811767578125, 0.00914764404296875, 0.0207061767578125, 0.0118255615234375, 0.01212310791015625, 0.005916595458984375, -0.0010213851928710938, 0.0023479461669921875, 0.021881103515625, 0.00012731552124023438, -0.01194000244140625, -0.002685546875, 0.036834716796875, 0.007099151611328125, 0.0110321044921875, 0.0288238525390625, -0.0153045654296875, -0.003139495849609375, -0.005069732666015625, 0.0259246826171875, 0.0242156982421875, 0.05328369140625, -0.0074005126953125, 0.0038661956787109375, -0.0249176025390625, -0.0215301513671875, 0.04266357421875, -0.022308349609375, 0.03436279296875, 0.004367828369140625, -0.04949951171875, -0.004852294921875, -0.001079559326171875, -0.004665374755859375, 0.01229095458984375, 0.0274810791015625, 0.0028667449951171875, -0.01024627685546875, -0.00699615478515625, 0.0014276504516601562, -0.0044708251953125, 0.006778717041015625, 0.01517486572265625, -0.007701873779296875, -0.0141754150390625, 0.01131439208984375, -0.022216796875, 0.00792694091796875, 0.00799560546875, 0.0009899139404296875, 0.02532958984375, -0.038726806640625, -0.01123809814453125, 0.0214691162109375, 0.01959228515625, 0.01392364501953125, -0.03155517578125, -0.00814056396484375, 0.0007276535034179688], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Delegating cognitive operations to generative artificial intelligence (AI) redistributes execution and raises a governance problem: specifically, it challenges how and where humans retain meaningful oversight and control over tasks performed by AI. While prior research on AI governance has focused on accountability, transparency, and the risks of automation, this article addresses a distinct issue: the timing and visibility of human direction within AI-assisted workflows. Our novel contribution is to examine not just whether humans are involved, but precisely when and how their input leaves observable traces during the delegation process\u2014an aspect often overlooked in existing literature.\n\nWe focus on two key concepts to clarify this perspective. \"Specified delegation\" refers to human direction placed before execution, such as providing detailed instructions, constraints, or criteria that delimit the task. For example, a user might instruct an AI to \"summarize this legal contract in plain English, highlighting any clauses related to liability.\" In contrast, \"iterative coproduction\" refers to human direction during production, through interventions that correct or redirect provisional outputs. For instance, a user might review an AI-generated draft and repeatedly revise sections in real time to clarify points or adjust tone. While these categories are analytically distinct\u2014specified delegation is akin to setting a recipe before cooking begins, and iterative coproduction is like tasting and adjusting the dish as it cooks\u2014real-world workflows may involve both forms simultaneously. For example, a user might provide detailed initial instructions (specified delegation) and then make ongoing corrections as the AI produces output (iterative coproduction). Edge cases include scenarios where initial instructions are refined mid-task, or where feedback loops blur the boundary between pre- and in-process direction. Recognizing these overlaps, our analysis treats the two concepts as endpoints on a spectrum of human-AI interaction, while empirically distinguishing their observable traces.\n\nTo streamline the narrative, we define two technical terms used throughout. \"Node-month pairs\" refer to unique combinations of user nodes (individual users or accounts) and months, allowing for longitudinal comparison. For example, if User A is active in both April and May, this yields two node-month pairs. \"Level-0 O*NET tasks\" are broad occupational task categories from the O*NET database, providing a standardized framework for comparing work-related activities across diverse users\u2014such as \"information input\" or \"decision making.\"\n\nOur analysis uses data from the Anthropic Economic Index (AEI) for April and May 2026. These months were selected because they represent the most recent period with complete and validated data at the time of analysis, providing a snapshot of current usage patterns. While no major seasonal or unique events were identified during this interval, the limited two-month window may not capture longer-term trends, rare events, or evolving user behaviors. Although internal monitoring suggests that usage patterns in April\u2013May 2026 are broadly consistent with adjacent months, the absence of a full year of data means that potential seasonal fluctuations, organizational cycles, or external shocks cannot be fully ruled out. Thus, while the period is likely representative of typical activity in the absence of known anomalies, it may miss fluctuations due to seasonality or other temporal factors. Future research should test for potential seasonality, event-driven effects, or shifts over longer timeframes to assess generalizability.\n\nThe AEI distinguishes two modes of use: 1P API, which corresponds to direct traffic through Anthropic's application programming interface (typically used by developers or organizations integrating AI into workflows), and Claude.ai, which combines activity from Chat and Cowork interfaces. The Chat interface is designed for conversational interactions with the AI, while the Cowork interface supports collaborative document editing and project management. This distinction is relevant because it reflects different user contexts and interaction patterns.\n\nWe next describe our methodological choices. We test whether a stronger work-related orientation of human-AI interaction is associated with more specified delegation within each mode, and whether the increase in the iterative profile is greater in Claude.ai than in 1P API. The main analysis uses level-0 O*NET tasks to ensure comparability across user types. We estimate how both profiles change when an eligible record reallocates ten percentage points from personal use to work-related use; this threshold was chosen because it represents a meaningful, observable shift in user intent\u2014such as a user spending one additional day per two-week period on work tasks rather than personal ones. This level of change is large enough to be practically significant, yet small enough to be observed in real-world usage patterns. While there is no universal standard for such thresholds, sensitivity analyses with alternative cutoffs (not shown) yielded qualitatively similar results, supporting the robustness of this choice. For comparability and to control for confounding differences between user groups, we restrict the iterative comparison to 1,411 node-month pairs that are observed and eligible in both modes during the study period. This ensures that the same users are compared across both interaction contexts.\n\nThe results show that specified delegation increases by 2.76 points in 1P API (95% CI: [2.30, 3.22]) and by 1.45 in Claude.ai (95% CI: [0.93, 1.97]) when work-related use rises by ten percentage points. To contextualize, these effect sizes are equivalent to moving from the 25th to the 50th percentile of specified delegation in the API context, or from a brief, general prompt to a moderately detailed instruction. For organizations, this could translate to clearer documentation of task requirements and more auditable records of human input, supporting compliance and accountability. On the common support, iterative coproduction changes by -0.30 points in 1P API and by 0.15 in Claude.ai, yielding a between-mode difference of 0.45 points (95% CI: [0.15, 0.75]). While modest, this is comparable to the difference between a user making no mid-task corrections and making one or two minor adjustments per session. This indicates that iterative, in-process human intervention is more responsive to work-related use in Claude.ai than in the API mode, suggesting that direct user interaction facilitates more real-time oversight. For users and organizations, this could mean that collaborative interfaces better support ongoing human correction and monitoring during AI-assisted work, potentially reducing errors or improving quality.\n\nThese findings show that work-related orientation is associated with stronger traces of prior human direction and that the observable iterative response varies across modes of use. However, the study is limited by its focus on a two-month period and aggregate data, which may not capture all nuances of user behavior. Additionally, the AEI dataset may be subject to selection biases, such as overrepresentation of certain user types or organizations, and distinguishing between personal and work-related use can be challenging due to overlapping activities or ambiguous user intent. These issues could lead to under- or overestimation of the true association between work orientation and delegation patterns. Future research could address these challenges by incorporating longer and more varied timeframes, collecting more granular user-level data, developing improved classification methods for user intent (e.g., through user surveys or behavioral validation), and employing sampling strategies to mitigate selection bias and account for additional confounders.\n\nIn conclusion, this article shifts attention from how much the AI executes to when human direction leaves observable traces, highlighting the importance of understanding the timing and form of human input for effective governance and oversight of AI-assisted work. These insights suggest that organizations and policymakers should consider not only the volume of AI involvement but also the mechanisms and moments of human oversight when designing governance frameworks and workplace practices for AI integration. Immediate next steps for practitioners include developing tools such as workflow logs or intervention dashboards that make human direction more visible and auditable at key stages of AI-assisted workflows, as well as implementing policies that require documentation of user interventions. Training programs should equip users to recognize, record, and reflect on their interventions, thereby supporting accountability and effective oversight in real-world settings.\n\n[Table 1: Conceptual distinctions and empirical findings]\n\n| Concept                | Definition/Example                                              | Observed Effect (per 10% work-use increase) |\n|------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------|\n| Specified Delegation   | Pre-task instructions (e.g., detailed prompt)                 | +2.76 (API), +1.45 (Claude.ai)             |\n| Iterative Coproduction | In-task corrections (e.g., real-time edits)                   | -0.30 (API), +0.15 (Claude.ai)             |\n| Overlap/Edge Case      | Both forms in a session (e.g., detailed prompt + live edits)  | See discussion                              |"}}